WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.670
Film Courage: If we take novel writing…so
are we going to be spending a lot of detail

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:12.450
on setting a world but if we’re doing a
screenplay how much are we really paying attention

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.570
to the where, the setting, the scenes?

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.610
Because won’t some of that be fleshed out
when the actual film starts and the director

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.200
storyboards or whatever?

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.740
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher
and Consultant: Yes, I think it is important

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.180
to pay attention to the where of your story
right?

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:32.369
Because every choice that you make in your
screenplay should be a deliberate one.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.780
If you’ve chosen a setting there should
have been some reasoning behind that.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.640
You’re not going to set your love story
about working with incarcerated writers, you’re

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.520
not going to set that in a juvenile detention
center.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.670
Film Courage: No.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:55.820
Naomi: You want it to be in a very specific
type of prison to inform that world.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.510
So I think you should pay attention to the
where.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:05.939
If we’re talking about the process of actually
conveying that on page.

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:13.749
I think the trick of screenwriting is to be
as concise as possible but as evocative as

00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:15.590
possible in those few words.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:22.490
That’s the trick of conveying it on the
page is being deliberate about your choice

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:28.819
and then conveying it as specifically as possible
so you can get as much out of your choice

00:01:28.819 --> 00:01:29.959
as you can.

00:01:29.959 --> 00:01:31.029
Does that make sense?

00:01:31.029 --> 00:01:39.249
Film Courage: It does, so fade in: If we’re
talking about this hypothetical library how

00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:41.119
much am I telling?

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:47.670
Naomi: So early in your screenplay because
you’re just introducing us to the world

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.590
of your story you will probably take a little
more time to convey where we are, the feel

00:01:53.590 --> 00:02:00.450
and the tone of where we are and maybe the
important characteristics of that setting.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:06.670
As your script progresses you will probably
need to describe less both because we’ve

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:12.069
probably seen a lot of these locations and
then also because you want your story to move

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:13.069
fast.

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:22.489
So as you go further along in the screenplay,
you’ll probably spend less time establishing

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:26.750
locations then you did in the very beginning
when you were trying to establish the world

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:31.659
and create that world in our minds so we could
live in it for the duration of your story.

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:38.000
Now that said I think that there is also value
in knowing which moments in your screenplay

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.760
should breath and when you have a moment that
you sort of need to give space to that might

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:51.621
be when you bring us into the world for a
little bit for a little bit longer for some

00:02:51.621 --> 00:02:55.050
extra detail or telling us what is going on
in that scene or something like that.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.080
Does that make sense?

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.950
Film Courage: It does.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.200
Is that the same if we’re doing a character
description in the beginning?

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.890
If we use this hypothetical librarian [a story
idea mentioned in an earlier part of this

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:12.310
interview], she’s age 35, straight blond
hair, maybe a little dark under the eyes from

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.100
staying up all night reading.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:23.540
Naomi: So there is a lot of emphasis put on
those character introductions right?

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.299
I have two minds about this.

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:30.190
I think that people get caught up in writing
like the perfect character introduction.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:34.140
I think that they are important because the
first time we meet your character we want

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:45.370
to give us a sense of who they are concisely
and evocatively but I’ve seen some character

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:51.920
introductions that are a little too long that
maybe stop the story in order to give us a

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.700
lot of information about the character the
moment we meet them which if you’re reading

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:04.180
a script it can really pull you out if you
are forced to stop the scene and meet this

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.180
person.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.310
That feels like it interrupts the flow of
the story right.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:14.450
I think the trick of writing character introduction
again is the same with all screenwriting which

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:20.209
is be as concise as you can but as evocative
as you can and what you’re trying to convey

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:25.970
when you introduce the character is give us
that defining quality about them, that essence

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.819
of the character that is going to be important
to the story.

00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:35.970
So physical descriptions might not be as useful
to us because the blond hair might never come

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.479
into play in the story.

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:42.560
But it might be useful to know that she’s
overworked or that she is burning the midnight

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.690
oil or that might be important to the story
and the situation that she finds herself in

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:53.660
so stuff like that could be useful and you
just have to determine what is the most defining

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:58.639
quality about this character that I need my
reader to understand right now as I introduce

00:04:58.639 --> 00:04:58.870
them

